    <!-- ========================================================
         X- AND Y- INTERCEPTS (VIDEO 4)
         ======================================================== -->

    <subsection xml:id="intercepts">
        <title>Identifying x- and y- Intercepts</title>

        <!-- Video 4: x- and y- Intercepts -->
        <p><url href="https://youtu.be/-08_QjVDu98">x- and y- Intercepts Video</url></p>

        <algorithm number="no">
            <title>Finding x- and y-intercepts</title>
            <ol>
                <li>To find the <m>x</m>-intercept(s), substitute <m>y=0</m> into the equation and solve for <m>x</m>.</li>
                <li>To find the <m>y</m>-intercept(s), substitute <m>x=0</m> into the equation and solve for <m>y</m>.</li>
            </ol>
        </algorithm>

        <p>
            Any point on the <m>x</m>-axis has <m>0</m> as its <m>y</m>-coordinate: <m>(a, 0)</m>.
            Any point on the <m>y</m>-axis has <m>0</m> as its <m>x</m>-coordinate: <m>(0, b)</m>.
        </p>

        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p>Estimate the <m>x</m>- and <m>y</m>-intercepts from the graph of <m>y = x^2 - 4</m> shown below.</p>
                <figure>
                    <caption>Graph of <m>y = x^2 - 4</m></caption>
                    <image xml:id="fig-intercepts-exercise" width="60%">
                        <shortdescription>Parabola y = x^2 - 4</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
                            <![CDATA[
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.52]
                                \draw[step=1,black!15,thin] (-5,-5) grid (5,5);
                                \draw[thick] (-5,0) -- (5,0);
                                \draw[thick] (0,-5) -- (0,5);
                                \draw[-latex,thick] (-5.35,0) -- (5.45,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                \draw[-latex,thick] (0,-5.35) -- (0,5.45) node[above] {$y$};
                                \foreach \x in {-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4}
                                    \draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node[below] {$\x$};
                                \foreach \y in {-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4}
                                    \draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y) node[left] {$\y$};
                                \draw[blue,very thick,domain=-3.1:3.1,samples=100] plot (\x,{\x*\x-4});
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                            ]]>
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>From the graph, the parabola crosses the <m>x</m>-axis at <m>(-2,0)</m> and <m>(2,0)</m>, and the <m>y</m>-axis at <m>(0,-4)</m>.</p>
                <figure>
                    <caption>Intercepts labeled</caption>
                    <image xml:id="fig-intercepts-solution" width="60%">
                        <shortdescription>Parabola with intercepts</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
                            <![CDATA[
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.52]
                                \draw[step=1,black!15,thin] (-5,-5) grid (5,5);
                                \draw[thick] (-5,0) -- (5,0);
                                \draw[thick] (0,-5) -- (0,5);
                                \draw[-latex,thick] (-5.35,0) -- (5.45,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                \draw[-latex,thick] (0,-5.35) -- (0,5.45) node[above] {$y$};
                                \foreach \x in {-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4}
                                    \draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node[below] {$\x$};
                                \foreach \y in {-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4}
                                    \draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y) node[left] {$\y$};
                                \draw[blue,very thick,domain=-3.1:3.1,samples=100] plot (\x,{\x*\x-4});
                                \fill[red] (-2,0) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (2,0) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (0,-4) circle (0.24);
                                \node[red,above] at (-2,0.3) {$(-2,0)$};
                                \node[red,above] at (2,0.3) {$(2,0)$};
                                \node[red,left] at (0,-3.7) {$(0,-4)$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                            ]]>
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </activity>

        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p>Find the <m>x</m>- and <m>y</m>-intercepts of the circle <m>(x + 3)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 16</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><em>Find the y-intercepts:</em> Set <m>x = 0</m>.</p>
                <me>(0 + 3)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 16</me>
                <me>9 + (y - 2)^2 = 16</me>
                <me>(y - 2)^2 = 7</me>
                <me>y = 2 \pm \sqrt{7}</me>
                <p>y-intercepts: <m>(0, 2 + \sqrt{7})</m> and <m>(0, 2 - \sqrt{7})</m>.</p>
                <p><em>Find the x-intercepts:</em> Set <m>y = 0</m>.</p>
                <me>(x + 3)^2 + (0 - 2)^2 = 16</me>
                <me>(x + 3)^2 + 4 = 16</me>
                <me>(x + 3)^2 = 12</me>
                <me>x = -3 \pm 2\sqrt{3}</me>
                <p>x-intercepts: <m>(-3 + 2\sqrt{3}, 0)</m> and <m>(-3 - 2\sqrt{3}, 0)</m>.</p>
                <figure>
                    <caption>Circle with intercepts</caption>
                    <image xml:id="fig-circle-intercepts-solution" width="60%">
                        <shortdescription>Circle with intercepts marked</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
                            <![CDATA[
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.48]
                                \draw[step=1,black!15,thin] (-8,-4) grid (4,8);
                                \draw[thick] (-8,0) -- (4,0);
                                \draw[thick] (0,-4) -- (0,8);
                                \draw[-latex,thick] (-8.35,0) -- (4.45,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                \draw[-latex,thick] (0,-4.35) -- (0,8.45) node[above] {$y$};
                                \foreach \x in {-7,-6,-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3}
                                    \draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node[below] {$\x$};
                                \foreach \y in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5,6,7}
                                    \draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y) node[left] {$\y$};
                                \draw[blue,very thick] (-3,2) circle (4);
                                \fill[red] (-7,2) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (1,2) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (-3,6) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (-3,-2) circle (0.24);
                                \draw[red,thick,fill=white] (-3,2) circle (0.24);
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                            ]]>
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </activity>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ========================================================
         CIRCLES (VIDEO 5)
         ======================================================== -->

    <subsection xml:id="circles">
        <title>Circles</title>

        <!-- Video 5: Circles -->
        <p><url href="https://youtu.be/JUvo3GrgWHk">Circles Video</url></p>

        <p>
            A circle is the set of all points that are the same distance from a fixed point called the <em>center</em>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Let the point <m>(h, k)</m> be the center, some value <m>r</m> for the radius, and begin with the distance formula.
        </p>

        <me>r = \sqrt{(x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2}</me>
        <me>r^2 = (x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2</me>

        <p>
            This is the <em>standard form</em> of an equation of a circle with center <m>(h, k)</m> and radius <m>r &gt; 0</m>.
        </p>

        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p><em>Example.</em> Graph the circle with center <m>(-3, 2)</m> and radius <m>4</m>.</p>
                <p>The equation is <m>(x + 3)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 4^2</m>.</p>
                <p>Mark the center with an open circle, then plot points 4 units up, down, left, and right from the center, and draw the circle.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Center: <m>(-3,2)</m>. Points:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Up: <m>(-3, 6)</m></li>
                    <li>Down: <m>(-3, -2)</m></li>
                    <li>Left: <m>(-7, 2)</m></li>
                    <li>Right: <m>(1, 2)</m></li>
                </ul>
                <p>The graph is shown below:</p>
                <figure>
                    <caption>Circle with center (-3,2) and radius 4</caption>
                    <image xml:id="fig-circle-example-solution" width="60%">
                        <shortdescription>Circle with center and radius marked</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
                            <![CDATA[
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.48]
                                \draw[step=1,black!15,thin] (-8,-4) grid (4,8);
                                \draw[thick] (-8,0) -- (4,0);
                                \draw[thick] (0,-4) -- (0,8);
                                \draw[-latex,thick] (-8.35,0) -- (4.45,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                \draw[-latex,thick] (0,-4.35) -- (0,8.45) node[above] {$y$};
                                \foreach \x in {-7,-6,-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3}
                                    \draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node[below] {$\x$};
                                \foreach \y in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5,6,7}
                                    \draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y) node[left] {$\y$};
                                \draw[blue,very thick] (-3,2) circle (4);
                                \fill[red] (-7,2) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (1,2) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (-3,6) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (-3,-2) circle (0.24);
                                \draw[red,thick,fill=white] (-3,2) circle (0.24);
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                            ]]>
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </activity>

        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p><em>Practice.</em> Write the equation of the circle with center <m>(-1, -2)</m> and radius <m>3</m> in standard form, then graph the circle.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The standard form is <m>(x - (-1))^2 + (y - (-2))^2 = 3^2</m>, i.e.</p>
                <me>(x + 1)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 9</me>
                <p>Graph: center at <m>(-1,-2)</m>, radius <m>3</m>.</p>
                <figure>
                    <caption>Circle with center (-1,-2) and radius 3</caption>
                    <image xml:id="fig-circle-center-1-2" width="60%">
                        <shortdescription>Circle with center (-1,-2) radius 3</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
                            <![CDATA[
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.52]
                                \draw[step=1,black!15,thin] (-5,-5) grid (5,3);
                                \draw[thick] (-5,0) -- (5,0);
                                \draw[thick] (0,-5) -- (0,3);
                                \draw[-latex,thick] (-5.35,0) -- (5.45,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                \draw[-latex,thick] (0,-5.35) -- (0,3.45) node[above] {$y$};
                                \foreach \x in {-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4}
                                    \draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node[below] {$\x$};
                                \foreach \y in {-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2}
                                    \draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y) node[left] {$\y$};
                                \draw[blue,very thick] (-1,-2) circle (3);
                                \draw[red,thick,fill=white] (-1,-2) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (-4,-2) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (2,-2) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (-1,1) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (-1,-5) circle (0.24);
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                            ]]>
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </activity>

        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p><em>Practice.</em> Write the equation of the circle with center at <m>(3, -2)</m> and tangent to the <m>y</m>-axis in standard form, then graph the circle.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    If the circle is tangent to the y-axis, the radius is the horizontal distance from the center to the y-axis.
                    The center is <m>(3, -2)</m>, so the distance to the y-axis is <m>3</m>. Thus, <m>r = 3</m>.
                </p>
                <p>The equation in standard form is:</p>
                <me>(x - 3)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 9</me>
                <p>Graph: center <m>(3,-2)</m>, radius <m>3</m>.</p>
                <figure>
                    <caption>Circle tangent to the y-axis</caption>
                    <image xml:id="fig-circle-tangent-solution" width="60%">
                        <shortdescription>Circle tangent to y-axis</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
                            <![CDATA[
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.52]
                                \draw[step=1,black!15,thin] (-3,-6) grid (7,4);
                                \draw[thick] (-3,0) -- (7,0);
                                \draw[thick] (0,-6) -- (0,4);
                                \draw[-latex,thick] (-3.35,0) -- (7.45,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                \draw[-latex,thick] (0,-6.35) -- (0,4.45) node[above] {$y$};
                                \foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5,6}
                                    \draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node[below] {$\x$};
                                \foreach \y in {-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3}
                                    \draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y) node[left] {$\y$};
                                \draw[blue,very thick] (3,-2) circle (3);
                                \draw[red,thick,fill=white] (3,-2) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (0,-2) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (3,1) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (3,-5) circle (0.24);
                                \fill[red] (6,-2) circle (0.24);
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                            ]]>
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </activity>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ========================================================
         GENERAL FORM OF A CIRCLE
         ======================================================== -->

    <subsection xml:id="general-form-circle">
        <title>General Form of a Circle</title>

        <p>
            The <em>general form</em> of an equation of a circle is:
        </p>
        <me>x^2 + y^2 + Ax + By + C = 0</me>

        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p><em>Example.</em> Write the equation <m>(x + 3)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 16</m> in general form.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><em>Step 1: Expand the squares.</em></p>
                <me>(x^2 + 6x + 9) + (y^2 - 4y + 4) = 16</me>
                <p><em>Step 2: Combine like terms and set equal to 0.</em></p>
                <me>x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 4y + 13 = 16</me>
                <me>x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 4y - 3 = 0</me>
                <p>Therefore, the general form is <m>x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 4y - 3 = 0</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </activity>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ========================================================
         COMPLETING THE SQUARE
         ======================================================== -->

    <subsection xml:id="completing-square-circle">
        <title>Completing the Square for Circles</title>

        <p>
            To write the equation of a circle from general form into standard form, we must complete the square twice.
        </p>

        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p><em>Example 1.</em> Given <m>x^2 + y^2 + 12x - 14y + 84 = 0</m>, write the equation in standard form and identify the center and radius.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><em>Step 1: Group x and y terms.</em></p>
                <me>(x^2 + 12x) + (y^2 - 14y) = -84</me>
                <p><em>Step 2: Complete the square for x.</em></p>
                <p>Half of <m>12</m> is <m>6</m>, square it: <m>36</m>.</p>
                <p><em>Step 3: Complete the square for y.</em></p>
                <p>Half of <m>-14</m> is <m>-7</m>, square it: <m>49</m>.</p>
                <p><em>Step 4: Add to both sides.</em></p>
                <me>(x^2 + 12x + 36) + (y^2 - 14y + 49) = -84 + 36 + 49</me>
                <p><em>Step 5: Factor.</em></p>
                <me>(x + 6)^2 + (y - 7)^2 = 1</me>
                <p>Therefore, the center is <m>(-6, 7)</m> and the radius is <m>r = 1</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </activity>

        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p><em>Example 2.</em> Complete the square to write <m>4x^2 - 12x + 4y^2 + 8y = 51</m> in standard form.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><em>Step 1: Factor out the coefficient of <m>x^2</m> and <m>y^2</m>.</em></p>
                <me>4(x^2 - 3x) + 4(y^2 + 2y) = 51</me>
                <p><em>Step 2: Complete the square for x.</em></p>
                <p>Half of <m>-3</m> is <m>-\frac{3}{2}</m>, square it: <m>\frac{9}{4}</m>.</p>
                <p><em>Step 3: Complete the square for y.</em></p>
                <p>Half of <m>2</m> is <m>1</m>, square it: <m>1</m>.</p>
                <p><em>Step 4: Add to both sides (inside the parentheses).</em></p>
                <me>4\left(x^2 - 3x + \frac{9}{4}\right) + 4(y^2 + 2y + 1) = 51 + 4\left(\frac{9}{4}\right) + 4(1)</me>
                <p><em>Step 5: Simplify.</em></p>
                <me>4\left(x - \frac{3}{2}\right)^2 + 4(y + 1)^2 = 51 + 9 + 4</me>
                <me>4\left(x - \frac{3}{2}\right)^2 + 4(y + 1)^2 = 64</me>
                <p><em>Step 6: Divide by 4.</em></p>
                <me>\left(x - \frac{3}{2}\right)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 16</me>
                <p>Therefore, the center is <m>\left(\frac{3}{2}, -1\right)</m> and the radius is <m>r = 4</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </activity>
    </subsection>

</section>